#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124193 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124579 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea4038bf-34f0-58cc-a7b6-6a4c1890570f" :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for an Unbounded Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a5050a89-0b53-5499-8a78-9d5d7c5a78c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2$.\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $2$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty$, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} f(x) = \\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124467 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cfbdc8f-6c4f-5411-b6f5-b7f0f046ac4f" :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for a Bounded Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f0e213c5-8a7a-51a3-995f-394270a22bd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3559" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ increases to $\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4$.\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the function $f(x)$ approaches $4$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) = 4\n$$"}]}
